well blood banks here in southern nevada  and across the nation are in dire need  for blood donations they are still  trying to level up for pre-pandemic  numbers victoria saha explains why it's  so important to donate regularly even as  you may never know when you or your  loved ones could need a blood  transfusion  at red cross southern nevada the donated  bags of blood on the shelves are a bit  of a relief for now blood can't sit on  the shelves for more than about 50 so 55  56 days so this comes through pretty  quickly rachel flanagan the executive  director of red cross southern nevada  says their worst fear is seeing the  shelves empty  just like they did last year some of  those folks they need up to 100  transfusions a year of the 60 percent of  the population eligible to donate she  says only 3 percent do we saw declines  over the summer people traveling and so  everybody's just kind of getting back to  school and doing all those things that  they need to do from trauma patients  surgeries cancer treatments blood is  used in every department for our local  hospitals i constantly see a need for it  long-time donor and hospital nurse  nicole bai says her first-hand  experience pushes her to donate blood  every eight weeks and there are times  where we have to wait  several hours days just to get them the  help that they need  donating is pretty simple and it really  just feels like a pinch but there are  some restrictions standing in the way  for potential donors the red cross's  website listing sexual identity getting  a tattoo in the past three months  certain diseases and a certain travel  outside the u.s as limiting factors  they have been offering more incentives  to get people to donate like gift cards  and t-shirts and no matter if you're a  first time donor or regular donor each  donation can save up to three lives  victoria saha 8 news now
 

